list*DA growable, contiguous sequence of `T`, owning its own heap storage.:::d*^Reading `v[i]`. Bounds-checked: an out-of-range index panics rather than
reading past the end.:8*2Borrowing `&mut v[i]`, with the same bounds check.:Ä*½Borrowing `&v[i]`, with the same bounds check. The read-only sibling of
`index_mut` above: same address, but a `cell[T]` rather than `cell_mut[T]`
so only `.get()` is offered, not `.set()`.:“*ŒReading `v[a..b]` â€” a read-only sub-view over the same storage, no copy.
A separate `index` impl from the element one above, with its own
`output`: indexing by a range means something different from indexing by
a position, and the two answer with different types.

`b` is exclusive and `b == self.len` is in bounds (an empty view at the
end is a legal, useful thing to ask for); `a > b` is not.:Ü*ÕBorrowing `&mut v[a..b]`. The mutable sibling of the impl above, and the
reason `index_mut` carries its own `output_mut`: this hands back a
